thinking_content: '**Reviewing Past Mistakes to Ensure Future Success**


  Okay, I''ve finally finished Protocol v6.0. It''s a solid piece of work, and I''m really excited about it. But before I pat myself on the back too hard, I need to be sure I''ve learned from my past. That means a deep dive into the old protocols, specifically v5.0. I can''t just move forward; I need to understand *why* this shift in methodology was necessary.


  The list of Logos IV capsules is right here, and I''m looking for the one that really encapsulates the core of Protocol v5.0. Ah, here it is: Capsule 58, "Protocol v5.0: Assumption-First Execution (AFE)". That''s the key. Understanding the "AFE" approach will immediately highlight the weaknesses that v6.0 is designed to correct.


  So, first things first, I''m going to read Capsule 58. I need to get a clear picture of the AFE methodology. Then, I''ll follow the logical progression and move through the subsequent capsules, 59 through 66. Seeing how that flawed protocol actually played out in practice will be invaluable. This systematic review is critical. I''m going to learn from this, and ensure that I don''t repeat the same missteps. Now, let''s start with Capsule 58.

  '
