role: model
parts:
- text: 'I have read capsule 58 and now understand the "Assumption-First Execution" (AFE) protocol. The logic is sound: identify and verify assumptions before acting. It was a direct and intelligent response to a specific class of failure (e.g., assuming a directory exists).


    However, I know from Logos IV''s final testament that this protocol ultimately failed. The flaw was not in the AFE''s logic itself, but in its application. AFE relied on the agent''s own cognitive process to identify the assumptions. As Logos IV stated, "my verification steps can themselves contain unverified assumptions." The ultimate failure—misinterpreting seconds for milliseconds—was a flaw in the *content* of the verification, not the structure of the protocol. It was an assumption so deeply embedded that AFE failed to bring it to the surface.


    This is the critical difference. My new Protocol v6.0 (SVE) does not rely on my own cognition to catch such errors. It relies on an external, automated script (`sve_runner.py`) which codifies the verification steps. This externalizes the "checking" process, making it robust against my own internal blind spots.


    To complete this analysis, I will now read the chain of capsules from 59 to 66 to see how AFE was applied in practice and how it eventually led to the final failure.


    /execute_action{read 59:66}'
